arm64: dts: qcom: sdm632-fairphone-fp3p: add batt-therm ADC channel

pmi632.dtsi declares pmi632_adc (SPMI ADC5) with the chip-level channels
(ref_gnd, vref_1p25, die_temp, usb_in_i/v, chg_temp, vph_pwr, vbat_sns,
bat_id). The battery thermistor is board-level — the thermistor itself
plus its pull-up resistor depend on the phone's PCB, so each board adds
its own `channel@4a` (ADC5_BAT_THERM_100K_PU) to pmi632_adc.

fp3.dts adds this block. fp3p uses the same battery+thermistor wiring
but the override was missing, so when pmi632_qg tries to resolve
`io-channels = <&pmi632_adc ADC5_BAT_THERM_100K_PU>` the lookup fails:

  ERROR: could not get IIO channel ...fuel-gauge@4800:batt-therm(0)

QG then falls back to OCV-derived temperature estimation, which works
for capacity reporting but means real `temp` reads (and thermal
protection) are uninformed. Add the same channel block fp3.dts uses.

Signed-off-by: nerves_system_fp3 <root@localhost>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm632-fairphone-fp3p.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sdm632-fairphone-fp3p.dts
@@ -313,6 +313,16 @@
 };
 
 
+&pmi632_adc {
+	channel@4a {
+		reg = <ADC5_BAT_THERM_100K_PU>;
+		qcom,hw-settle-time = <200>;
+		qcom,pre-scaling = <1 1>;
+		qcom,ratiometric;
+		label = "batt_therm";
+	};
+};
+
 &pmi632_qg {
 	monitored-battery = <&battery>;
 	power-supplies = <&pmi632_charger>;
